CaTCHseq pipeline
================

Overview
--------
The CaTCHseq pipeline processes PCR-amplified CaTCH libraries from FASTQ files through mapping, barcode collapsing/deduplication, multiplet handling, and reporting. It is built with Nextflow (DSL2) and supports CellRanger or STARsolo mapping. Deduplication leverages umi_tools network-based clustering with tunable strategies and distances for CaTCH barcodes and UMIs.

Quick start (Docker-based defaults)
-----------------------------------
```bash
nextflow run nextflow/main.nf \
	--libraries <libraries.csv> \
	--outputDir ./CaTCHseq_OUTPUT \
	--reportsDir ./REPORTS
```

Key inputs
----------
- `--libraries` (CSV, required): columns SampleName, Condition, Replicate, LibraryType (GEX|CaTCHseq), R1, R2, CellNumber, Chemistry (e.g. 10X, ScaleBio, PIPseq).
- Reference inputs vary by mapper:
	- CellRanger: `--index` (transcriptome), `--reference`, `--annotation` as needed for building indexes.
	- STARsolo: `--index`, `--reference`, `--annotation`, optional `--whitelist`.

PIPseq libraries
----------------
PIPseq libraries are converted to 10X compatible FASTQ with the `pipspeak` tool before mapping. The bundled configuration is the VBCF configuration `configs/20261107` of commit `e2524c786f33c1a73650c865eb82e5a0601a50d9` of the in-house repository https://ngs.vbcf.ac.at/repo/software/pipspeak.git, copied verbatim into `nextflow/conf/pipseq/pipspeak.yaml`. That repository derives from the MIT licensed upstream https://github.com/noamteyssier/pipspeak (Copyright (c) 2023 Noam Teyssier); the upstream licence and attribution apply to the derived source.

- `pipspeak.yaml` (source name `20250716_config.yaml`) referencing `bc1_custom_primer.txt`, `bc2.txt` and `bc3.txt` (96 barcodes of 8 nt each, 24 nt concatenated barcode) with the spacers `AACC` and `ACAG`.
- Read 1 layout: 8 nt phase, barcode 1, `AACC`, barcode 2, `ACAG`, barcode 3, `CCTATTCGAG`, 12 nt UMI. `--offset 8` is a search window of up to 8 nt from the start of R1 in which a barcode is accepted, not a required exactly 8 nt phase, so reads with a shorter phase still match. pipspeak runs with `--offset 8 --umi-offset 10 --umi-len 12` and emits `<prefix>_R1.fq.gz` with the corrected 24 nt barcode plus 12 nt UMI, the unchanged passing R2, `<prefix>_log.yaml`, `<prefix>_whitelist.txt` (barcode plus UMI, not the barcode alone) and the QC TSVs.

Select PIPseq through the chemistry column of the samplesheet:

```
SampleName,Condition,Replicate,Lane,LibraryType,R1,R2,CellNumber,Chemistry
PIPseqDay0,Day0,1,1,GEX,/data/pip_GEX_R1.fastq.gz,/data/pip_GEX_R2.fastq.gz,5000,PIPseq
PIPseqDay0,Day0,1,1,CaTCHseq,/data/pip_CaTCH_R1.fastq.gz,/data/pip_CaTCH_R2.fastq.gz,NA,PIPseq
```

The samplesheet checker requires the same chemistry for all rows of a sample (GEX, CaTCHseq and lanes), paired `.fastq.gz` files for PIPseq rows and a library type of `GEX` or `CaTCHseq`.

The `preprocessPipseq` process validates that every lane is paired and has the same number of R1/R2 records, merges the lanes in lane order, runs pipspeak on a task owned copy of the configuration and validates that the converted R1/R2 are non empty and paired before mapping. GEX libraries are additionally trimmed with `cutadapt -A AGATCGGAAGAGCGTCGTGTAGGGAAAGAGTGT --minimum-length 20`; CaTCHseq R2 reads are passed on untrimmed.

Settings (defaults in `nextflow/conf/default-params.config`):

- `--pipseq_config` (optional, default `nextflow/conf/pipseq/pipspeak.yaml`). The default YAML ships with the barcode lists it references, so a run with the bundled chemistry needs no flag. Pass `--pipseq_config` only for a customized chemistry; every barcode list is resolved as a path relative to the directory of the selected YAML, so `../sibling/list.txt` and nested paths are allowed while absolute paths are rejected. Only the selected YAML and the lists it references are staged into the task work directory, each list under its own indexed directory, so the task reads a task owned copy, the originals are never modified and the Nextflow cache keys on the staged content. Barcode lists must have uniform length and sum to `--pipseq_cb_length`.
- `--pipseq_cb_length` (24), `--pipseq_umi_length` (12), `--pipseq_offset` (8), `--pipseq_umi_offset` (10), `--pipseq_exact` (false). A `parameters.umi_len` in the YAML overrides the CLI value inside pipspeak, so the helper rejects a mismatch instead of silently using the YAML value.
- `--pipseq_clip3p` (0) adds `--clip3pNbases` to STARsolo for PIPseq libraries.
- `--pipseq_container` (default `ghcr.io/obenauflab/pipspeak:latest`, the published image). To run a locally rebuilt image instead, build `catchseq-pipspeak:e2524c7` with `docker build -t catchseq-pipspeak:e2524c7 docker/pipspeak` and pass `--pipseq_container catchseq-pipspeak:e2524c7`. The image compiles pipspeak from `docker/pipspeak/source.tar.gz` with `--locked` against the tracked `docker/pipspeak/Cargo.lock` and adds cutadapt 5.0 and PyYAML 6.0.2.

Building the PIPseq image
-------------------------
`docker/pipspeak/source.tar.gz` is a local build input and is not tracked; `.gitignore` excludes it. Regenerate it from an authorized checkout of the VBCF repository at the pinned commit and build the image:

```bash
git -C /path/to/vbcf-pipspeak archive e2524c786f33c1a73650c865eb82e5a0601a50d9 Cargo.toml src LICENSE README.md | gzip > docker/pipspeak/source.tar.gz
docker build -t catchseq-pipspeak:e2524c7 docker/pipspeak
```

The archive does not need to carry `Cargo.lock`: the Dockerfile copies the tracked `docker/pipspeak/Cargo.lock` over the extracted source before `cargo build --release --locked`, so the checked-in lock is authoritative. The image is published as `ghcr.io/obenauflab/pipspeak:latest` and can be pulled without an authorized source checkout, so running the pipeline needs no VBCF repository access. Rebuilding the image from source still requires an authorized checkout of the VBCF repository; reproducibility is not claimed without that source access.

Running the PIPseq preprocessing without a container
----------------------------------------------------
`preprocessPipseq` calls `docker/scripts/python/preparePipseq.py`, which needs `pipspeak`, `cutadapt` and PyYAML. `pipspeak` is not packaged on conda-forge or bioconda (the package API returns 404 for both), so there is no `conda install pipspeak` and no `pip install pipspeak`; those dependencies are only available through the image or a source build. A conda-forge environment can supply the rest (`python=3.12`, `rust`, `pip`, then `pip install cutadapt==5.0 PyYAML==6.0.2` to match the image). For a pinned native build, export the source to a temporary directory, copy the tracked lock next to it and install into the environment prefix only:

```bash
git -C /path/to/vbcf-pipspeak archive e2524c786f33c1a73650c865eb82e5a0601a50d9 | tar -x -C /tmp/pipspeak-src
cp docker/pipspeak/Cargo.lock /tmp/pipspeak-src/Cargo.lock
cargo install --locked --path /tmp/pipspeak-src --root "$CONDA_PREFIX"
```

That installs `$CONDA_PREFIX/bin/pipspeak` without a global install or a change to the base environment; `preparePipseq.py --pipspeak-bin` points the helper at it.

To run the pipeline natively, disable the container engines through a small external config instead of editing the tracked configs, and point the script paths at the checkout:

```bash
cat > /tmp/native.config <<'EOF'
docker.enabled = false
apptainer.enabled = false
singularity.enabled = false
podman.enabled = false
shifter.enabled = false
charliecloud.enabled = false
EOF
PYTHONPATH=/abs/repo/docker nextflow -c /tmp/native.config run nextflow/main.nf \
	--libraries <libraries.csv> \
	--scriptDirPy /abs/repo/docker/scripts/python/ \
	--scriptDirR /abs/repo/docker/scripts/R/ \
	--outputDir ./CaTCHseq_OUTPUT \
	--reportsDir ./REPORTS
```

`--scriptDirPy` and `--scriptDirR` must end with a slash because the process scripts concatenate the file name. `PYTHONPATH=/abs/repo/docker` makes the shared `docker/` Python modules importable. `--binDir` only prefixes the `fastqc` and `multiqc` executables used by `--withQC`; set it to a directory that actually holds them, or leave `--withQC` off. Only the PIPseq preprocessing step has been exercised this way; the remaining steps still expect the tools and libraries of the main image, so a fully container-free run is not claimed as tested.

Mapping and counting: PIPseq is mapped with STARsolo only (`--mapper STAR`); any other mapper stops the run with `PIPseq CellRanger integration not implemented/verified; use --mapper STAR`. STARsolo runs with `--soloCBstart 1 --soloCBlen 24 --soloUMIstart 25 --soloUMIlen 12 --soloStrand Forward --soloBarcodeReadLength 0 --soloCellFilter EmptyDrops_CR --soloUMIdedup 1MM_CR --soloUMIfiltering MultiGeneUMI_CR` and `--soloCBwhitelist None`; the pipspeak whitelist is not used for mapping. CaTCH barcode counting reuses the same geometry (barcode start 0, length 24, UMI start 24, length 12).

Scope: PIPseq support in CaTCHseq is intended for paired GEX and CaTCHseq libraries. GEX-only analysis belongs in the standalone scRNA-seq pipeline, not in a separate CaTCHseq mode.

Major parameters
----------------
The authoritative list (including defaults) is `nextflow run nextflow/main.nf --help`; the same set is echoed at the start of every run. The full list:
- Output locations and code paths
	- `--outputDir` (default `./CaTCHseq_nf_OUTPUT`) and `--reportsDir` (default `./REPORTS`).
	- `--scriptDirR`, `--scriptDirPy`, `--binDir`: script/binary paths; keep the defaults (`/tools/scripts/R/`, `/tools/scripts/python/`, `/usr/local/bin/`) when running with Docker, otherwise point `--scriptDirR` and `--scriptDirPy` at the checkout (`<repo>/docker/scripts/R/`, `<repo>/docker/scripts/python/`, trailing slash included). `--binDir` only prefixes the `fastqc` and `multiqc` executables.
- General
	- `--mapper` (CellRanger|STAR) and mapper-specific params `--cellranger_params`, `--star_params`, `--idx_params`.
	- `--withQC` to run FastQC/MultiQC; `--fastqc_params` for extra options.
	- `--chunkSize` read chunking for barcode counting.
	- `--minReads` minimum reads per CaTCH barcode; `--filter` to use filtered counts.
	- `--stopOnWarnings` stop the pipeline when a task warns (default true); `--help` prints the usage message.

- PIPseq preprocessing (see the PIPseq section above)
	- `--pipseq_config` (optional, default `nextflow/conf/pipseq/pipspeak.yaml`).
	- `--pipseq_cb_length` (default 24), `--pipseq_umi_length` (default 12), `--pipseq_offset` (default 8), `--pipseq_umi_offset` (default 10), `--pipseq_exact` (default false).
	- `--pipseq_clip3p` (default 0) and `--pipseq_container` (default `ghcr.io/obenauflab/pipspeak:latest`).

- Cell calling / CaTCH barcode classification
	- `--min_detected_barcodes` (default 10) minimum CaTCH barcode reads per cell.
	- `--singlet_cutoff` (default 0.8), `--bc1_cutoff` (default 0.4), `--bc2_cutoff` (default 0.3) classify cells as Singlet / Double_Integration.
	- `--stringency` (default|stringent|lenient, default default) fixed-elements filter applied to the raw barcode sequence.

- Barcode/UMI collapsing (umi_tools-backed)
	- Distance cutoffs:
		- `--maxDist` global fallback (default 1).
		- `--maxDistCaTCH` distance for CaTCH barcode collapsing (default 1; falls back to `--maxDist`).
		- `--maxDistUMIs` distance for UMI collapsing (default 1; falls back to `--maxDist`).
	- Network methods:
		- `--clusterMethodCaTCH` (directional/adjacency/cluster, default directional).
		- `--clusterMethodUMIs` (directional/adjacency/cluster, default directional).
	- Uniqueness toggle: `--uniqueCaTCH` (true/false) to enable umi_tools-based collapsing; otherwise Hamming distance.
	- `--majorityVote` (default 90) share of reads the most frequent barcode needs to resolve a multiplet.

- Cell filtering, normalisation and object outputs
	- `--max_mt_percent` (default 10), `--min_detected_features` (default 500), `--hvg_cutoff` (default 0.1) filter cells and call highly variable genes.
	- `--output_seurat` (default true), `--output_sce` (default false), `--output_anndata` (default true) select which single-cell object types are produced and published.
	- `--run_sct` (default false) run SCTransform normalisation and SCT reductions/clustering instead of LogNormalize.
	- `--organism` (Human|Mouse) used for pathway analysis of DE genes; `--marker` RDS file with cell-cycle stage markers.

- CaTCH barcode differential enrichment
	- `--de_method` (barbieq|deseq2, default barbieq) selects the analysis method. `deseq2` runs the legacy DESeq2/edgeR analysis.
	- barbieQ computes per-sample diversity (Shannon/Simpson) and dominance (Simpson's D, Berger-Parker) indices and tests differential barcode proportions of every condition against the reference condition (`--baseline`, default `Day0`; the legacy param name `--refName` is still accepted).
	- `--de_min_count` (default 10, 0 disables) drops barcodes whose summed count within the two contrasted conditions is below the cutoff; applied per comparison.
	- `--de_min_replicates` (default 2) minimum replicates in a condition required to run differential testing.
	- `--de_all_vs_all` additionally tests every condition against every other condition into a separate table.
	- `--de_rds` optional precomputed barbieQ `.rds` to reuse instead of rebuilding the object, needs to be an absolute path accessible from the task.
	- `--pval_cutoff` (default 0.1) and `--lfc_cutoff` (default 1) set the p-value and log-fold-change cutoffs for the DE plots and tables.
	- As before, the barcode DE analysis runs once per sample tag over all its conditions and replicates, and is skipped entirely when fewer than two conditions are present.

Pipeline steps (high level)
---------------------------
1) **QC** (optional): FastQC → MultiQC summaries.
2) **Mapping**: CellRanger count or STARsolo (with chemistry-specific presets).
3) **Barcode counting**: Count CaTCH barcodes in chunks and merge.
4) **Collapse & filter**: Apply distance/method settings to deduplicate CaTCH barcodes and UMIs; remove background.
5) **Multiplet resolution**: Majority-vote based merging of multiplets.
6) **Reports & tables**: Generate CaTCH barcode and cell summaries plus analytics plots.

PIPseq libraries get one extra step before QC and mapping: `preprocessPipseq` runs pipspeak on the merged lanes and adapter trims the GEX R2 reads.

Chemistry presets
------------------
STARsolo options are defined once in `nextflow/conf/chemistry.config` under `params.chemistries` and resolved by `nextflow/lib/Chemistry.groovy`. Each entry declares a `mode`, a one-based `geometry` (`cbStart`, `cbLen`, `umiStart`, `umiLen`, plus `barcodeRead` where the barcode sits in R2) and an ordered `starOptions` map. The geometry is authoritative: the `--soloCBstart/--soloCBlen/--soloUMIstart/--soloUMIlen` options are taken from it, and `countBarcodesInChunks` derives its 0-based `--bcStart/--bcLength/--umiStart/--umiLength` from the same values, so mapping and counting cannot drift apart.

Supported names in the `Chemistry` column: `10X` (generic, 16 bp CB + 12 bp UMI, the default for any name containing `10X` that is not listed below), `10Xv2` (16 + 10), `10Xv3` (16 + 12), `Droplet` (16 + 10), `ScaleBio` (29 + 8, barcode read 2), `Smart` (SmartSeq, no barcode geometry) and `PIPseq` (24 + 12). `10x`, `droplet`, `scalebio`, `smart`, `smartseq` and `pipseq` are accepted as aliases.

Override a preset in a `-c` config; assignments merge into the defaults, so only the changed fields need to be given:

```groovy
params {
    chemistries {
        '10Xv2' {
            geometry {
                umiLen = 10
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The legacy overrides `star_10X`, `star_droplet`, `star_scalebio`, `star_smart` and `star_pipseq` default to `null` and are applied when set: a string replaces the preset options and its `--soloCB*`/`--soloUMI*` values also become the counting geometry; `star_pipseq` additionally accepts a `{ cbLen, umiLen -> ... }` closure. `--pipseq_cb_length` and `--pipseq_umi_length` change the converted PIPseq geometry and the UMI start follows the barcode length; the raw preprocessing geometry (`--pipseq_offset`, `--pipseq_umi_offset`) is separate and unchanged.

CLI mapping for collapse script
-------------------------------
- Nextflow params map to `collapseCaTCHbarcodes.py`:
	- `--clusterMethodCaTCH` → `--cluster-method-catch`
	- `--clusterMethodUMIs`  → `--cluster-method-umis`
	- `--maxDist`            → `--maxdist`
	- `--maxDistCaTCH`       → `--maxdist-catch`
	- `--maxDistUMIs`        → `--maxdist-umis`
	- `--uniqueCaTCH`        → `--unique`
	- `--minReads`           → `--minsupport`
- All default to the original behaviour (directional, distance 1) if not set.

Helper scripts
--------------
- Every Python script in `docker/scripts/python/` and every R script in `docker/scripts/R/` (including `docker/scripts/R/optional/`) exposes its own `--help`/usage message listing all accepted options with defaults; run e.g. `collapseCaTCHbarcodes.py --help` or `Rscript preprocessData.R --help`.
- The pipeline invokes these scripts with the option values derived from the Nextflow params listed above, so the Nextflow options are the user-facing interface.

Outputs
-------
- `OUTPUT/Counts/` – intermediate and collapsed `.sclib` libraries and stats.
- `OUTPUT/Reports/` – tables (`*.CaTCHbarcodes`, `*.cells`) and plots.
- `OUTPUT/CellRanger/` or `OUTPUT/STAR/` – mapper-specific outputs.

Tips
----
- Provide chemistry-specific parameters for STARsolo if deviating from 10X presets.
- Ensure `--libraries` paths are accessible where Nextflow executes (local or workdir-mounted in containers).
